# PySpark DataFrame – Practical Notes with Industry Real Project Example

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Industry Use Case – Sales Analytics Project

This example is based on a real-world industry scenario.

Business Problem:
- Company wants to analyze sales data
- Calculate revenue
- Identify high-value customers
- Generate reports

Data Sources:
- Orders data
- Customers data

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Orders DataFrame (Source System)

Columns:
- order_id
- customer_id
- product
- quantity
- price

Create Orders DataFrame:

orders_data = [
    (1, 101, "Laptop", 1, 50000),
    (2, 102, "Mobile", 2, 20000),
    (3, 101, "Mouse", 3, 500),
    (4, 103, "Keyboard", 1, 1500),
    (5, 104, "Monitor", 2, 8000)
]

orders_columns = ["order_id", "customer_id", "product", "quantity", "price"]

orders_df = spark.createDataFrame(orders_data, orders_columns)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Orders DataFrame Output (df.show())

+--------+------------+--------+--------+------+
|order_id|customer_id |product |quantity|price |
+--------+------------+--------+--------+------+
|1       |101         |Laptop  |1       |50000 |
|2       |102         |Mobile  |2       |20000 |
|3       |101         |Mouse   |3       |500   |
|4       |103         |Keyboard|1       |1500  |
|5       |104         |Monitor |2       |8000  |
+--------+------------+--------+--------+------+

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Customers DataFrame (Master Data)

Columns:
- customer_id
- customer_name
- city

Create Customers DataFrame:

customers_data = [
    (101, "Rahul", "Mumbai"),
    (102, "Anita", "Pune"),
    (103, "Suresh", "Delhi"),
    (105, "Neha", "Bangalore")
]

customers_columns = ["customer_id", "customer_name", "city"]

customers_df = spark.createDataFrame(customers_data, customers_columns)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Customers DataFrame Output (df.show())

+------------+-------------+----------+
|customer_id |customer_name|city      |
+------------+-------------+----------+
|101         |Rahul        |Mumbai    |
|102         |Anita        |Pune      |
|103         |Suresh       |Delhi     |
|105         |Neha         |Bangalore |
+------------+-------------+----------+

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Add Derived Column – Total Amount

Business Logic:
- total_amount = quantity * price

Transformation:

orders_enriched_df = orders_df.withColumn("total_amount", orders_df.quantity * orders_df.price)

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Output After Adding total_amount

+--------+------------+--------+--------+------+------------+
|order_id|customer_id |product |quantity|price |total_amount|
+--------+------------+--------+--------+------+------------+
|1       |101         |Laptop  |1       |50000 |50000       |
|2       |102         |Mobile  |2       |20000 |40000       |
|3       |101         |Mouse   |3       |500   |1500        |
|4       |103         |Keyboard|1       |1500  |1500        |
|5       |104         |Monitor |2       |8000  |16000       |
+--------+------------+--------+--------+------+------------+

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Join Orders with Customers (Left Join)

Purpose:
- Attach customer details to each order
- Orders should not be lost

Join Logic:

final_df = orders_enriched_df.join(customers_df, "customer_id", "left")

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Joined DataFrame Output

+------------+--------+--------+--------+------+------------+-------------+--------+
|customer_id |order_id|product |quantity|price |total_amount|customer_name|city    |
+------------+--------+--------+--------+------+------------+-------------+--------+
|101         |1       |Laptop  |1       |50000 |50000       |Rahul        |Mumbai  |
|101         |3       |Mouse   |3       |500   |1500        |Rahul        |Mumbai  |
|102         |2       |Mobile  |2       |20000 |40000       |Anita        |Pune    |
|103         |4       |Keyboard|1       |1500  |1500        |Suresh       |Delhi   |
|104         |5       |Monitor |2       |8000  |16000       |null         |null    |
+------------+--------+--------+--------+------+------------+-------------+--------+

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Business Insight – Customer Revenue

Requirement:
- Calculate total revenue per customer

Aggregation:

revenue_df = final_df.groupBy("customer_name") \
    .sum("total_amount")

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Revenue Output

+-------------+------------------+
|customer_name|sum(total_amount) |
+-------------+------------------+
|Rahul        |51500             |
|Anita        |40000             |
|Suresh       |1500              |
|null         |16000             |
+-------------+------------------+

------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Real Industry Observations

- NULL customer indicates missing master data
- Revenue calculation done using aggregation
- Join type selection impacts final output
- Derived columns are common in ETL pipelines

